,2): slope = (2-1)/(3-2) = 1, allowed.\n- (2,1) to (3,3): slope = (3-1)/(3-2) = 2, allowed.\n\n- (2,2) to (3,1): slope = (1-2)/(3-2) = -1, not allowed.\n- (2,2) to (3,2): slope = 0, not allowed.\n- (2,2) to (3,3): slope = 1, allowed.\n\n- (2,3) to (3,1): slope = (1-3)/(3-2) = -2, allowed.\n- (2,3) to (3,2): slope = (2-3)/(3-2) = -1, not allowed.\n- (2,3) to (3,3): slope = 0, not allowed.\n\n- (2,4) to (3,1): slope = (1-4)/(3-2) = -3, allowed.\n- (2,4) to (3,2): slope = (2-4)/(3-2) = -2, allowed.\n- (2,4) to (3,3): slope = (3-4)/(3-2) = -1, not allowed.\n\nThus possible connections for sunny lines (non-horizontal, non-vertical, non -1 slope) are:\n\n- L1: (2,1)-(3,2) slope 1\n- L2: (2,1)-(3,3) slope 2\n- L3: (2,2)-(3,3) slope 1\n- L4: (2,3)-(3,1) slope -2\n- L5: (2,4)-(3,1) slope -3\n- L6: (2,4)-(3,2) slope -2\n\nWe need to assign each column 2 point to a line, and each column 3 point to a line (some lines may not have a column 3 point). Let's try to assign:\n\n- Use (2,1)-(3,2) line L1 (slope 1). This covers (2,1) and (3,2).\n- Use (2,2)-(3,3) line L2 (slope 1). This covers (2,2) and (3,3).\n- Use (2,3)-(3,1) line L3 (slope -2). This covers (2,3) and (3,1).\n- Use (2,4) left for line L4, which may not need a column 3 point (since we already covered all three column 3 points). So L4 will cover (2,4) and maybe column 4 or 5.\n\nNow we need to cover column 4 points: (4,1),(4,2). We have four lines L1-L4. L1 currently passes through (2,1) and (3,2). Could it also pass through (4,3)? Let's check: slope = 1, line through (2,1) has equation y = x - 1. At x=4, y=3, not a point in T_n (since (4,3) sum=7>6). So L1 cannot cover column 4 points. L2 passes through (2,2) and (3,3). Equation: y = x? Actually line through (2,2) slope 1 => y = x. At x=4, y=4, not in T_n (4+4=8>6). So L2 cannot cover column 4 points. L3 passes through (2,3) and (3,1). Slope = (1-3)/(3-2) = -2. Equation: y - 3 = -2 (x - 2) => y = -2x + 7. At x=4, y = -8 + 7 = -1, not in T_n. So L3 cannot cover column 