e non-sunny line for k=4. So we could use a non-sunny line of slope -1 to cover n points (one per column). Then we have 4 sunny lines to cover the rest. That might be enough.\n\nLet's try for n=5: Use non-sunny line L0: x+y = 6 (i.e., points (1,5),(2,4),(3,3),(4,2),(5,1)). This covers one point per column, all columns. Then we have 4 sunny lines to cover the remaining points. The remaining points are all points except those on the main diagonal. Let's list them:\n\nColumn 1: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4) (since (1,5) covered)\nColumn 2: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3) (since (2,4) covered)\nColumn 3: (3,1),(3,2) (since (3,3) covered)\nColumn 4: (4,1) (since (4,2) covered)\nColumn 5: none (since (5,1) covered)\n\nNow we have 4 sunny lines to cover these points. Column 1 has 4 points, column 2 has 3 points, column 3 has 2 points, column 4 has 1 point. So total points left = 10. Each sunny line can cover at most one point per column, so each can cover at most 4 points (since there are 4 columns). So total capacity = 4*4 = 16 > 10, so possible. We need to assign each column's points to the 4 lines.\n\nColumn 1 has 4 points, so each sunny line must cover exactly one point in column 1. So each line will be assigned a distinct point from column 1: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4). Then each line can also cover a point in column 2, maybe column 3, column 4.\n\nWe need to find 4 lines that each pass through a distinct point in column 1 and also pass through some points in other columns.\n\nLet's try to assign:\n\n- L1: (1,1) and (2,2) maybe? Slope = (2-1)/(2-1) = 1, allowed. So L1: y = x. This line also passes through (3,3) which is already covered by non-sunny line, but that's okay. It also passes through (4,4) not in T_n, (5,5) not in T_n. So L1 covers (1,1),(2,2). Good.\n\n- L2: (1,2) and (3,1) maybe? Slope = (1-2)/(3-1) = -1/2 = -0.5, allowed. So L2: passes through (1,2),(3,1). It also passes through (5,0) not in T_n. So L2 covers (1,2),(3,1).\n\n- L3: (1,3) and (2,1) maybe? Slope = (1-3)/(2-1) = -2