dy used (vertical x=1 and diagonal). Could we replace vertical line with something else to free up a non-sunny line for (2,4)? Let's think.\n\nWe used vertical line x=1 (non-sunny) covering column 1. Could we instead use a diagonal line x+y=7 (non-sunny) covering many points, and maybe a vertical line x=2 (non-sunny) covering column 2? That would free up column 2 points to be covered by non-sunny line, reducing the load on sunny lines. Let's try a different approach: Use non-sunny lines: vertical x=1 and vertical x=2 (both non-sunny). Then we have 4 sunny lines left to cover columns 3-6. Let's see.\n\nVertical x=1 covers column 1 (6 points). Vertical x=2 covers column 2 (5 points). Remaining points:\n\nColumn 3: (3,1)-(3,4) (4 points)\nColumn 4: (4,1)-(4,3) (3 points)\nColumn 5: (5,1)-(5,2) (2 points)\nColumn 6: (6,1) (1 point)\n\nTotal remaining points = 4+3+2+1 = 10. We have 4 sunny lines to cover them. Each sunny line can cover at most one point per column (columns 3-6). So each line can cover at most 4 points. So capacity = 4*4 = 16 > 10, possible.\n\nNow we need to assign points from columns 3-6 to 4 lines.\n\nColumn 3 has 4 points, so each line must take exactly one point from column 3. So each line will have a distinct point from column 3: (3,1),(3,2),(3,3),(3,4). Column 4 has 3 points, so three lines will also take a point from column 4: (4,1),(4,2),(4,3). Column 5 has 2 points, so two lines will also take a point from column 5: (5,1),(5,2). Column 6 has 1 point, so one line will also take (6,1). So we need to find 4 lines that each contain a point from column 3 and possibly also points from columns 4,5,6, such that each line is sunny.\n\nLet's try to find lines:\n\n- L1: (3,1) and (4,2) slope = (2-1)/(4-3) = 1, sunny. This line also passes through (5,3) not in T_n (since column 5 max row is 2). So L1 covers (3,1),(4,2).\n\n- L2: (3,2) and (5,1) slope = (1-2)/(5-3) = -1/2 = -0.5, sunny. This line also passes through (4,1.5) not integer. So L2 covers (3,2),(5