nes to cover the remaining columns. Because the three rightmost columns already force the use of a vertical line for column n-2, we would run out of vertical lines before all columns are covered.\"\n\nWe need to check if this claim is mathematically correct. Let's analyze the logic.\n\nWe have n columns. Each sunny line can cover at most one point per column. So across all k sunny lines, each column can have at most k points covered by sunny lines. For columns with more than k points, we need non-sunny lines to cover the remaining points. Non-sunny lines can be vertical (cover whole column), horizontal (cover at most one point per column), or slope -1 (cover at most one point per column). So to cover a column with more than k points, we need at least one vertical line (or multiple non-sunny lines, but vertical is the only one that can cover more than one point in a column). Actually horizontal lines can also cover at most one point per column, but they can be used to cover points across columns. So to cover a column with h_c > k points, we need at least h_c - k non-sunny lines that intersect that column (each covering at most one point). But we have only n - k non-sunny lines total. So we need to allocate them across columns.\n\nThe solution's argument seems to assume that for each column with h_c > k, we need at least one vertical line. But that's not necessarily true: we could use multiple non-sunny lines (horizontal or slope -1) to cover the extra points, each covering one point in that column. However, those lines also cover points in other columns, so they could be used efficiently. So the claim that we need a vertical line for each column with h_c > k is too strong. For k≥4, columns with h_c > k are those with h_c >=5. For n≥5, column 1 has h_1 = n, which is > k for k=4 if n≥5. So column 1 has many points. We could cover column 1's points using vertical line x=1 (non-sunny) or using multiple non-sunny lines (horizontal or slope -1) each covering one point. But