n- Column n-1: none (both covered)\n- Column n: none (covered)\n\nThus uncovered points: (n-3,1),(n-3,2),(n-2,1). That's three points. We have used V=n-4, D=2 => total lines used = n-4+2 = n-2. So we have 2 lines left, both sunny. We need to cover these three points with two sunny lines. As before, any line connecting any two of these points is either vertical (n-3,1)-(n-3,2) vertical, horizontal (n-3,1)-(n-2,1) horizontal, or slope -1 (n-3,2)-(n-2,1) slope -1. So cannot. So we need three sunny lines. So fails.\n\nThus V=n-5, D=2, S=2 => total = n-5+2+2 = n-1, need one more line (maybe diagonal). But we can try V=n-5, D=3, S=2 => total = n. Let's test.\n\nVertical lines x=1,...,n-5. Diagonal lines: x+y=n-1, x+y=n, x+y=n+1. Let's see.\n\nColumns left: n-4,n-3,n-2,n-1,n. Points in these columns:\n\n- Column n-4: b ≤ 5 => (n-4,1) sum n-3, (n-4,2) sum n-2, (n-4,3) sum n-1, (n-4,4) sum n, (n-4,5) sum n+1.\n- Column n-3: b ≤ 4 => sums n-2, n-1, n, n+1.\n- Column n-2: b ≤ 3 => sums n-1, n, n+1.\n- Column n-1: b ≤ 2 => sums n, n+1.\n- Column n: b ≤ 1 => sum n+1.\n\nWe have diagonal lines for sums n-1, n, n+1. They cover:\n\n- x+y=n+1: points (n-4,5),(n-3,4),(n-2,3),(n-1,2),(n,1) => 5 points.\n- x+y=n: points (n-4,4),(n-3,3),(n-2,2),(n-1,1) => 4 points.\n- x+y=n-1: points (n-4,3),(n-3,2),(n-2,1) => 3 points.\n\nThus diagonal lines cover all points in columns n-4,...,n except (n-4,1) sum n-3 and (n-4,2) sum n-2. So uncovered points: (n-4,1) and (n-4,2). That's two points in column n-4. We have used V=n-5, D=3 => total lines used = n-5+3 = n-2. So we have 2 lines left, both sunny. We need to cover (n-4,1) and (n-4,2) with two sunny lines. Since they are vertical, each sunny line can cover at most one of them (since any line passing through both is vertical, not allowed). So we can use one sunny line to cover (n-4,1) and another sunny line to cover (n-4,2). That's fine. So we have S=2. So total lines = n-5 + 3 + 2 = n. So we have a covering with exactly 2 sunny lines for any n≥