um=7, covered.\n- (3,2) sum=5, not covered.\n- (3,3) sum=6, covered.\n- (3,4) sum=7, covered.\n- (4,2) sum=6, covered.\n- (4,3) sum=7, covered.\n- (5,2) sum=7, covered.\n- (5,1) covered by row 1 and diagonal.\n- (6,1) covered.\n\nThus uncovered points: (2,2),(2,3),(3,2). That's three points. Two sunny lines cannot cover all three (any line connecting any two is either vertical/horizontal/slope -1). So fails.\n\nThus many combos fail.\n\nThus it seems k=2 may be impossible for n≥6. Let's try to prove impossibility for k≥2 for n≥6? The solution claims k≥4 impossible by column counting. But maybe k=2 also impossible for n≥6. Let's try to reason.\n\nWe need to cover all points with n lines, exactly k sunny lines. The non-sunny lines are vertical, horizontal, diagonal (slope -1). Let's denote V, H, D counts. The total number of non-sunny lines is n-k. So we have n-k lines that are vertical/horizontal/diagonal. The total number of points is n(n+1)/2. Each non-sunny line can cover at most n points (vertical/horizontal/diagonal). So total coverage capacity of non-sunny lines is at most (n-k)*n. The sunny lines can also cover at most n points each (since any line can intersect at most n points of the triangle? Actually a line can intersect at most n+1 points? Let's check: The triangle has points with a+b ≤ n+1. A line of arbitrary slope can intersect at most n+1 points? Let's think: The maximum number of points on a line in this set is n (for vertical/horizontal/diagonal) or maybe n+1 for some other slope? Let's examine: The line y = -x + (n+1) passes through points (1,n),(2,n-1),...,(n,1) which are n points (since a from 1 to n). So diagonal lines have n points. A vertical line x=i passes through points (i,b) for b=1,...,n+1-i, which is at most n points (when i=1). Horizontal line y=j passes through points (a,j) for a=1,...,n+1-j, also at most n points (when j=1). So any line can have at most n points. So each line can cover at most n points. So total coverage capacity of n